     4	Tool-level hooks are NOT configurable from a bundle. The YAML schema has `before_tool_callbacks` / `after_tool_callbacks` (and the
     5	model/agent variants), but they are lists of NAMES looked up in a pre-approved CallbackRegistry (adk_submission/schema.py:253-254,
     6	296-299; adk_submission/registry.py:217-246). `resolve_callback_fields` returns no kwargs when `ctx.callback_registry is None`
     7	(adk_submission/resolvers/callbacks.py:84-85), and the harness calls `compile_submission(...)` without a `callback_registry`
     8	(swegemma/harness/agent_runner.py:616-628), so any callback a bundle names is silently dropped. No bundle-level code, tool wrapper
     9	or tool-response hook exists. What a bundle CAN do is declare a sub-agent as a tool (`agent_tool`, adk_submission/schema.py:91-137).
    10	The harness builds one tool set per task from the task Context (`tools = context.create_tools()`, agent_runner.py:601) and passes
    11	it to the compiler for every agent, so a sub-agent's `submit_patch` is the same function on the same Context. It sets
    12	`ctx.patch_submitted = True` (swegemma/tools/execution.py:111-112), and the runner ends the session on that flag
    13	(agent_runner.py:920). Consequences: (i) the empty-diff guard can be an IN-BUNDLE sub-agent gate. The root gets an `agent_tool` named
    14	`submit_patch` holding `run_command` plus the real `submit_patch`, and it ships unchanged to the scorer. Its enforcement is
    15	the gate model's compliance with a fixed 4-step instruction (temp 0), not code. (ii) The repeat guard can't intercept arbitrary
    16	tool calls: wrapping every tool in a sub-agent would cost an LLM turn per call. So it is PROMPT-LEVEL. Nothing is patched
    17	into our local harness copy. Assumption: the scorer runs the same swegemma 0.2.11 (docs/GOLD_CALIBRATION.md:104). The smoke
    18	(below) verifies the gate end to end in the Kaggle kernel.
